course: everyday
lesson: 76
title: Washing your body and caring for your hair
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Our skin sweats every day, and sweat with dirt and germs starts to smell, so we wash all over with soap two or three times a week at least, and every day when we have been sweaty.
explain: Bathrooms have hidden dangers: very hot water scalds, wet floors are slippery, water and electricity together can kill, and a small child can drown in a few centimetres of water.
explain: Some things are only for one person: towels, combs, brushes and hats, because germs, skin infections and head lice spread through them.
explain: Head lice are tiny insects that live in hair; anyone can get them, clean or dirty, and they are easy to treat with a fine-tooth comb.
explain: A girl in Sydney runs her bath with the cold water first, then the hot, and an adult tests it with an elbow before she gets in, so she never gets scalded.
fact: Washing all over with soap removes sweat, dirt and smell, at least two or three times a week and every day when sweaty.
fact: Bath water is run cold first, then hot, and an adult checks it with an elbow, which feels heat better than a hand, because hot water can scald skin badly.
fact: Wet bathroom floors are slippery, so you step out of the bath or shower carefully onto a bath mat.
fact: Behind the ears, under the arms, the feet and the private parts get sweaty, so they are washed with soap, and toes are dried well to stop fungus.
fact: A small child must never be left alone in a bath, because small children can drown in a few centimetres of water in seconds.
fact: Electrical things like hair dryers are never used near water, because water and electricity together can kill.
fact: Towels can spread germs and skin infections, so everyone uses their own.
fact: Clean underwear and socks are worn every day, because they hold sweat and germs.
fact: A short shower of about five minutes saves water and energy.
fact: Hair is washed with shampoo when it looks or feels greasy, often two or three times a week, and rinsed well.
fact: Tangled hair is brushed gently from the ends upwards, because pulling from the top hurts and breaks hair.
fact: Head lice (people often call them nits) spread through shared combs, brushes and hats and touching heads, and they are not a sign of being dirty.
fact: Children do not cut their own hair, because it often goes wrong and scissors near the eyes are dangerous.
exercise: How often should a child wash all over? | At least two or three times a week, and every day when sweaty | Only once a month, as washing more often dries out the skin badly | Only when someone says you smell | Three times every day
exercise: How should a bath be run? | Cold water first, then hot, checked by an adult with an elbow | Hot water first, then jump in quickly before it has time to cool | Only hot water, so it stays warm longer | Test it by putting your face in it
exercise: Why is stepping out of the bath carefully onto a mat important? | Wet floors are slippery and bathroom falls are common | The mat keeps the bath water warm for the next person who uses it | It stops the bath from overflowing | Bath mats dry your feet completely
exercise: Which parts of the body especially need soap? | Behind the ears, under the arms, the feet and the private parts | Only the hands and the face | Only the parts you can see in the mirror | Only the hair and the knees
exercise: Why should you dry well between your toes? | Damp skin there can grow fungus | Toes shrink and wrinkle up for good if they stay wet | Wet toes make socks too tight | It helps the toenails grow
exercise: Your little brother is in the bath and your mum leaves the room. What should you do? | Call an adult, a small child must never be alone in a bath | Leave him, the water is not deep enough for anything to happen | Go and play, he can swim | Let the water out while he is still in
exercise: Why must a hair dryer never be used near a full sink or bath? | Water and electricity together can kill | The steam ruins the hair dryer's colour | The hair dryer warms the water too much | It makes the hair wet again
exercise: Why should you not share a towel? | Towels can spread germs and skin infections | Towels shrink a lot when two people use them | Shared towels dry more slowly | Your towel would get lost
exercise: After your bath, which clothes should you put on? | Clean underwear and socks | Yesterday's socks if they look clean | The same underwear for the whole week | Any clothes from the floor
exercise: Why should showers be kept short? | It saves water and energy | Long showers wash off your skin | Short showers make you cleaner | Long showers make the water cold for ever
exercise: When should you wash your hair? | When it looks or feels greasy, often two or three times a week | Only once a year | Every hour on hot days, with lots of shampoo each time you wash it | Never, hair cleans itself
exercise: How should you brush tangled hair? | Gently, starting from the ends and working upwards | Hard from the top all the way down in one strong pull | Only when it is dry and very tangled | By cutting the tangles out with scissors
exercise: Why should you not share combs and hats? | Head lice and scalp infections spread that way | They lose their shape when they are shared with others | Other people's hair is the wrong colour | It is rude to touch another person's hat
exercise: A note comes home about head lice and your head itches. What should you do? | Tell a parent so they can check with a fine-tooth comb | Hide it so nobody knows | Shave all your hair off yourself | Wash your hair in very hot water on your own, again and again
exercise: A classmate has head lice. How should you treat him? | Kindly, without teasing, anyone can get lice | Tease him, because lice mean he is dirty | Stay away from him for the whole school year | Tell everyone in the school
exercise: Your fringe is in your eyes. What should you do? | Ask an adult or a hairdresser to cut it | Cut it yourself in front of a mirror | Cut it quickly with the big kitchen scissors | Pull the hairs out one by one
test: A boy in Texas played football all afternoon in the heat. What should he do that evening? | Have a wash or shower with soap | Just change his shirt | Wait until the weekend to wash | Spray on deodorant instead of washing
test: Which is true about head lice? | They like clean hair as much as dirty hair | Only dirty children who never wash get them | They jump from far away like fleas | They cannot be treated
test: A two-year-old is sitting in only a little bath water. Is it safe to leave her for a minute? | No, a small child can drown in a few centimetres of water in seconds | Yes, the water is far too shallow for a child to come to any harm | Yes, if she is sitting down | Yes, if the door is left open
test: A girl wants to dry her hair while standing next to a full bath. What is the danger? | If the dryer touches the water, the electricity could kill her | The hot air could make all the bath water boil and burn her | The steam would break the dryer slowly | There is no danger if the dryer is new
test: What would probably happen if a boy wore the same socks all week? | They would hold sweat and germs and start to smell | They would get a little cleaner each day he wore them | His feet would grow faster | Nothing, socks do not get dirty
test: Why does an adult test bath water with an elbow and not a hand? | The elbow feels heat better, so it is a fairer test | The hand is always too dirty to put in the bath | The elbow cannot get scalded | Elbows are the strongest part of the arm and do not mind heat
test: Two friends at a sleepover want to use one towel to save washing. What is better? | Each uses their own towel | They share it but use different sides | They share it if it looks clean | They dry themselves with their pyjamas
test: Why do people brush long tangled hair from the ends first? | Pulling from the top hurts and breaks the hair | The ends are the dirtiest part | It makes the hair grow longer and thicker | It is the only way to see the knots
test: A family wants to use less water. What helps in the bathroom? | Shorter showers of about five minutes | Longer baths with more bubbles | Leaving the tap running while brushing | Showering three times a day
test: running the bath for my baby bro, i'll pop out for a sec | Never leave a small child alone in the bath | It is fine for a minute or two, nothing happens that fast | It is fine if the water is shallow | It is fine if he can sit up
test: can i dry my hair with the hairdryer while im in the bath | No, electricity near water can kill | Yes, if you hold it up high, well away from the water | Yes, if your hands are dry | Yes, if the bath is nearly empty
test: hot tap first then cold yeah | Run cold first, then hot, and an adult checks it | Hot first so it fills up faster | Only hot, it cools down by itself in a bit | It does not matter which tap first
test: same socks 3rd day, theyre fine | Wear clean socks every day, they hold sweat and germs | Socks are fine for a whole week as long as they don't smell yet | Only change them if they have holes | Turn them inside out instead
explain: A boy in Florida rarely showered after football; his feet got an itchy, cracked fungus between the toes that took weeks of cream to clear.
explain: A girl in Manchester had head lice but did not tell anyone because she was embarrassed; they spread to her sister and three friends before they were treated.
fact: Athlete's foot is a fungus that grows in warm, damp skin, so drying between your toes after washing helps stop it.
fact: Head lice are common in schools, are not caused by being dirty, and spread from head to head until they are treated.
test: toes itchy n peeling after footy, its nothin | It may be athlete's foot, wash, dry between toes and tell a parent | It means your feet are still growing | Wear the same wet socks to stop it | Itching means the skin is healing
test: think i got nits, too embarrassed to tell mum | Tell her anyway, lice are common and spread until treated | Keep it secret, they go away on their own after a week or two | Share a hat so they move away | Cut your own hair very short
test: skipped showers all week cos its winter | You still need to wash, sweat and dirt cause smells and sore skin | In winter you never get dirty | Cold weather kills all germs | Only wash when it is hot outside
test: my mum's mate said a toddler drowned in the bath when the mum went to get a towel for like 1 minute, what's the real lesson | Never leave a small child alone in a bath, not even briefly | Only deep baths are dangerous for toddlers | Toddlers can swim, so baths are always safe | Bath time is safe if the water is warm
test: kids reckon if u get nits ur head has to be shaved bald, true?? | Exaggerated; nits are treated with a fine-tooth comb | True, shaving it all off is the only cure there is for nits | True, and you must stay home for a year | False, nits go away by themselves overnight
test: everyone's sayin Lily got nits cos her family's filthy, should i tell people | No, it's unkind and untrue; anyone can get lice | Yes, everyone needs to know they are dirty | Yes, but only to her best friends | Yes, and tell people to stay away from her
exam: Identify the principal reason for which bath water is run cold first and tested by an adult before a child enters. | Hot water may scald the skin severely | Cold water makes the bath fill more quickly | Hot water causes the soap to stop working | Cold water first keeps the bath cleaner
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises head lice? | They spread from head to head and are not a sign of being dirty | They appear only on persons who never wash | They are caused by eating too many sweets | They are present only in the hair of adults
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning electrical appliances such as hair dryers. | They must never be used near water, as the two together can kill | They are safe near water provided the hands are wet | They may be used in the bath if switched to low | They are safe near water if the cord is long
exam: Determine the principal reason for which towels, combs and hats are not shared between persons. | Germs, skin infections and head lice spread through them | Shared towels become wet too quickly to use | Combs and hats wear out more swiftly when shared | Sharing such items is forbidden by school rules alone
